ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: MCAS Neurological Overlap - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Supple neck, negative meningeal signs, and absence of true fever, despite subjective feeling of heat/flushing.

Subarachnoid Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual crescendo onset over 3 hours alongside prominent systemic flushing, rather than an instantaneous "thunderclap" presentation.

Classic Migraine - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of distinct allergic/mast cell features (severe flushing, temporal association with known triggers) though the acute management overlaps.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, unremitting headache and neurological slowing, inherently requiring an urgent physical assessment to definitively exclude life-threatening intracranial emergencies like meningitis or hemorrhage. Upon confirming intact focal neurology and observing the classical mast cell flushing, it was determined the patient was suffering an acute neuro-inflammatory crisis driven by mast cell mediators. Urgent administration of parenteral anti-inflammatory and antihistamine agents was required to chemically abort the neurogenic wind-up.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ketorolac 30mg IM and Promethazine 25mg IM. This combination provides potent anti-inflammatory action while blocking H1 receptors and providing sedation to break the sensory hypersensitivity. Targeted intervention strategy includes Amitriptyline, PEA, and LDN.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly maintaining a cool, dark, and silent environment for the next 12 hours without any screen time, you will actively starve your overactive nervous system of sensory triggers, directly lowering the brain inflammation and rewarding yourself with restorative sleep.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Ice cap or cold compress to the forehead recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to discuss daily H1/H2 blockade and neuro-modulators.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to Emergency Department via 000 if patient develops a stiff neck preventing chin-to-chest movement, sudden projectile vomiting, or unilateral weakness/numbness.

